  "title": "Glaucoma predicted effector genes (Weeks EM et al. 2023)",
  "bodyHtml": "<p>This table displays gene prioritization results for glaucoma, as ascertained from a combination of Phecode annotation and self-report, from the study:</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Leveraging polygenic enrichments of gene features to predict genes underlying complex traits and diseases.<br />\r\nWeeks EM, Ulirsch JC, <i>et al.</i><br />\r\nNat Genet. 2023 Aug;55(8):1267-1276. doi: 10.1038/s41588-023-01443-6.<br />\r\nPMID: <a href=\"https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/37443254\" target=\"_blank\"> 37443254</a></p>\r\n\r\n<p>The results displayed below were downloaded from the <a href=\"https://www.finucanelab.org/data\" target=\"_blank\">Finucane lab website</a>.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>The authors developed a similarity-based gene prioritization method, Polygenic Priority Score (PoPS), to generate a gene-level score reflecting the likelihood that a gene near a genetic association locus is the effector gene in the locus for that trait. They applied this method to GWAS summary statistics and fine-mapping results from the UK Biobank (<a href=\"https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2021.09.03.21262975v1\" target=\"_blank\">Kanai <em>et al</em>., 2021</a>) for multiple complex traits, along with 7 additional gene prioritization methods.</p>\r\n\r\n<p>Mouse over the column headers for more details about each evidence type.</p>\r\n",
